'''Cosmocazo''' (from "[[W:Cosmopolitanism|Cosmopolitan]]" and the [[W:Spanish nouns#Other suffixes|''-azo'' suffix]] of violent augmentation), also known as the '''cosmopolitan era''', is the historiographic denomination given to the social, political and cultural movement that took place in [[Balisca]] following decades of increased [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration]] and the resulting process of [[W:Transculturation|transculturation]]. Reformers during this era, known as [[Cosmopolitanism in Balisca|Cosmopolitans]], sought to address issues they associated with rapid [[Technological and industrial history of Balisca|industrialization]], [[Urbanization in Balisca|urbanization]], immigration, and [[Corruption in Balisca|political corruption]], as well as increasing [[Trade unions in Balisca|labor radicalism]] in response to the consolidation of industrial ownership in [[W:Monopoly|monopolies]]. Reformers expressed concern about slums, [[Poverty in Balisca|poverty]], and labor conditions. Multiple overlapping movements pursued social, political, and economic reforms by advocating changes in governance, [[W:Scientific method|scientific methods]], and professionalism; regulating business; [[W:Environmental protection|protecting]] the [[W:Natural environment|natural environment]] and [[W:Indigenous decolonization|indigenous sovereignty]]; and seeking to improve urban living and working conditions. By the late-19th century there was an emergence of a [[W:Cosmopolitanism|cosmopolitan]] and [[W:Permissive society|permissive]] state based on the idea of [[W:Cultural pluralism|cultural pluralism]] for a [[W:Multiculturalism|diversity of communities]], based on the traditional [[Cerdanya|Cerdanyan]] concept [[W:seny|''seny'']] ("sense"). 

Its main form of expression was the adoption of the [[Baliscano language]] and "Cosmopolitan architecture", which led to steady progress on systemic improvements throughout it's cities using iron and glass as the main building materials and density-oriented urban planning. However it also encompassed many other arts, such as fashion, painting and sculpture, especially the design and the [[W:Decorative arts|decorative arts]] (cabinetmaking, carpentry, forged iron, ceramic tiles, ceramics, [[W:Glass-making|glass-making]], silver and goldsmith work, etc.), which were particularly important in their role as support to architecture. It was also a literary movement (poetry, fiction, drama).

Cosmopolitanism and {{W|cantonalism}} were important influences for those that became frustrated with the increasingly gaudy and extravagant displays of wealth by industrialists, and disillusioned with the tendency of {{W|caesarism}} by politicians of the [[Anyos Federales|''Anyos Federales'']]-era. 

== Background and history ==
In the [[Anyos Federales|post-civil war]] period, the relatively large immigrant-descended population was often economically marginalized and locked into relatively dangerous elicit means of survival until the emergence of railroads, [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]], and universal education which strengthened the integration of rural Balisca in the late-1800s. Decentralization happened gradually, while the cantons came together in 1884 to establish the [[Association of the Baliscan Language]] ({{lang-bl|L'Asociación de la Llengua Balisca}}), which set to reconcile and standardize the emerging [[Alpujarra|Alpujarran]] dialect into one standardized language, to be called [[Baliscano]]. In the process, the work of these linguists furthered the idea of a {{W|plurinationalism|plurinationalist}} Balisca, based on the interdependence of the cosmopolitan communities that had come to the Baliscan archipelago. 

Following the [[Cantonal War|Cantonal rebellions]], the cantonalists built upon policies that leaned toward a [[W:Horizontalidad|horizontal]], [[W:Anti-clerical|anti-clerical]] system of [[W:Democratic socialism|democratic socialism]], including the {{W|separation of church and state}}, [[W:Self-governance|self-policing]], the abolition of rent, the abolition of {{W|child labor}}, and the [[W:workers' self-management|right of employees to take over an enterprise]]. Active (predominately Catholic) churches and religious schools declined substantially due to their prior support for the presidency of [[Josèp Bernat|Bernat]], in contrast with most communities which were more receptive to the cantonal cause. {{W|Feminist}}, {{W|socialist}}, [[W:Communism|communist]] and [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] currents played important roles in the shaping of this new system.

{{W|Secular humanism}} and {{W|anti-clericalism}} witnessed surges in interest during this time. Later, the idea of {{W|cosmopolitanism}} entered mainstream Baliscan society. ''La Cosmópolis'' by [[Marc Soler]] greatly influenced national thought, furthering the idea of the new republic being a state of numerous ethnic groups and calling for historical reconciliation and opposition to sectarianism. This was enshrined in the Constitution of 1883, which defined Balisca as the first "cosmopolitan state". Also within the Constitution's articles was the recognition of [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous sovereignty]], environmental conservation and protections, the prohibition of {{W|religious schools}}, and sweeping land reforms.

